"Student laboratory · Calculus for physics",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Calculus</em>, Vol. {#}, §§{#} and {#}, and Vol. {#}, §{#} (Numerical Integration); OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (Impulse and Collisions). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/calculus-volume-{#} and https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Left and right sums on a rising curve; Signed area: sin x; Simpson is exact for a cubic; Area between √x and x²; F′ = f: the area so far; The bell curve: no formula for F; {#}/x² out to infinity: area {#}; {#}/x out to infinity: no limit; and, under In physics, Work stretching a spring; Impulse of a kick; Charge from a discharging capacitor; Displacement and distance from velocity; A measured force pulse: trapezoids.",
"Type a function in the <strong>f(x) =</strong> box, or switch the box to <strong>A data table</strong> and type readings, one x, y pair a line. The upper graph shows the function and fills the area from <strong>a</strong> to <strong>b</strong> with the pieces of the chosen rule.",
"In the bar above the graph, beside <strong>Play</strong> and <strong>Reset</strong>, the rule buttons (<strong>Left</strong>, <strong>Right</strong>, <strong>Midpoint</strong>, <strong>Trapezoid</strong>, <strong>Simpson</strong>) pick the rule, and <strong>Pieces n</strong> sets the number of pieces (type it in the box, or use −{#}, +{#} and × {#}; the slider under <strong>The sum</strong>, below the graphs, steps through set values). Simpson’s rule needs n even; an odd n is raised by one, and the note under the slider says so. <strong>Limits</strong> sets a and b.",
"The <strong>Lower graph</strong> buttons switch the graph under the picture: <strong>Error against n</strong> plots the size of each rule’s error against n on log axes, with the slope of each rule’s line in the key at the top right; <strong>F(x), the area so far</strong> and <strong>Area as b grows</strong> follow the area itself.",
"Readouts: <strong>Sum</strong> (the rule’s sum), <strong>Integral</strong> (the exact value, and under it how it was found), <strong>Error, sum − integral</strong>, and <strong>Error ratio, n to 2n</strong> (the error at n divided by the error at 2n).",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> stores the setup, the rule, n, the sum, the integral and the error. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the rows to Table {#} (formula readings) or Table {#} (table readings, when every row is from a data table); <strong>Clear</strong> starts a new series.",
"<strong>What the demo computes.</strong>",
"Each sum is computed exactly as its formula says. The exact integral is F(b) − F(a) from an antiderivative found by the basic rules; where there is none, it is computed numerically to about {#} significant figures, and the readout says so.",
"The force plate readings in the table setup are simulated, and labeled so: computed values, not measurements.",
"Measure how fast the left, midpoint, trapezoid and Simpson sums close in on an exact integral, find each rule’s order from the slope of its error on log axes, and then use the trapezoid rule on a table of force readings to find the impulse of a kick.",
"<strong>Riemann sums.</strong> To find the area under f(x) from a to b, cut [a, b] into n pieces of width \\(h = (b - a)/n\\), with edges \\(x_i = a + ih\\). Each rule adds up h times a height for each piece:",
"<strong>Left:</strong> \\(L_n = h\\,[f(x_0) + f(x_1) + \\dots + f(x_{n-1})]\\), the height at the left edge of each piece.",
"<strong>Midpoint:</strong> \\(M_n = h\\,[f(m_1) + \\dots + f(m_n)]\\), the height at the middle \\(m_i\\) of each piece.",
"<strong>Trapezoid:</strong> \\(T_n = \\tfrac{h}{2}\\,[f(x_0) + 2f(x_1) + \\dots + 2f(x_{n-1}) + f(x_n)]\\), straight lines joining the points.",
"<strong>Simpson</strong> (n even): \\(S_n = \\tfrac{h}{3}\\,[f(x_0) + 4f(x_1) + 2f(x_2) + 4f(x_3) + \\dots + 4f(x_{n-1}) + f(x_n)]\\), a parabola through each pair of pieces.",
"As n grows, every one of these closes in on the definite integral \\(\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx\\). They differ in how fast.",
"<strong>Order of a rule.</strong> For a smooth function and large n, the error of a rule behaves like a power of {#}/n,",
"\\[\\text{error} \\approx \\frac{C}{n^{p}},\\]",
"and p is the <strong>order</strong> of the rule. Doubling n divides the error by \\(2^p\\). Taking logarithms, \\(\\log\\lvert\\text{error}\\rvert = \\log\\lvert C\\rvert - p\\log n\\): on log scales the error against n is a straight line of slope −p. From two readings with \\(n_1\\) and \\(n_2\\) and errors \\(e_1\\) and \\(e_2\\), the observed order is",
"\\[p = \\frac{\\ln(\\lvert e_1\\rvert/\\lvert e_2\\rvert)}{\\ln(n_2/n_1)}.\\]",
"The leading errors are known (OpenStax Vol. {#}, §{#}):",
"\\[L_n - I \\approx -\\frac{(b-a)\\,[f(b) - f(a)]}{2n},\\qquad T_n - I \\approx \\frac{(b-a)^2\\,[f'(b) - f'(a)]}{12\\,n^2},\\qquad M_n - I \\approx -\\frac{(b-a)^2\\,[f'(b) - f'(a)]}{24\\,n^2},\\]",
"and Simpson’s error falls as \\(1/n^4\\). So the left rule is first order, midpoint and trapezoid second order, and Simpson fourth order. The midpoint error is half the trapezoid error and of opposite sign, which is why \\(S_{2n} = (2M_n + T_n)/3\\) cancels both.",
"<strong>An integral in physics.</strong> A force F(t) that acts for a short time gives an object an <strong>impulse</strong>, the area under the force against time,",
"\\[J = \\int F\\,dt,\\]",
"in N·s, equal to the change in its momentum (University Physics §{#}). A force plate does not give a formula for F(t); it gives readings every millisecond. Between readings nothing is known, so the natural estimate joins the readings with straight lines: the trapezoid rule over the table,",
"\\[J \\approx \\sum_i \\tfrac12\\,(F_i + F_{i+1})\\,(t_{i+1} - t_i).\\]",
"The left rule is first order and Simpson’s rule is fourth order. By what factor should each one’s error fall when n goes from {#} to {#}? From {#} to {#}?",
